    "final_answer": "I have successfully found and read the BBC article \"A really simple guide to climate change\". Here is the answer based on the article's section \"How are humans causing climate change?\":\n\n## Human Activities Causing Climate Change\n\nAccording to the BBC article, the rapid warming of the planet over the last century has been **without doubt caused by human activities**. Specifically:\n\n### The primary cause:\n- **Widespread use of fossil fuels** — coal, oil, and gas — in **homes, factories, and transport systems**.\n\n### How fossil fuels cause warming:\n- When fossil fuels burn, they **release greenhouse gases**, mostly **carbon dioxide (CO2)**.\n- This CO2 acts like a blanket, **trapping extra energy in the atmosphere near the Earth's surface**, causing the planet to heat up.\n\n### Evidence:\n- Since the start of the **Industrial Revolution** (when humans began burning large amounts of fossil fuels), the **amount of CO2 in the atmosphere has risen by more than 50%**, far above levels seen in the Earth's recent history.\n- The CO2 released from burning fossil fuels has a **distinctive chemical fingerprint** that matches the type of CO2 increasingly found in the atmosphere.\n\nIn summary, the article identifies the burning of fossil fuels (coal, oil, and gas) for energy in homes, factories, and transport systems as the main human activity driving climate change, with carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions being the key greenhouse gas responsible for trapping heat in the atmosphere.",
